If the project's AGENTS.md (included below when present) states its own branch naming or commit message convention, follow it instead of the defaults above.

Stage the changes that make up the fix, including any files it created, and nothing else. Leave out artifacts left behind by running tests or builds.

Push with `git push origin <branch-name> --no-verify` and read the outcome before doing anything else.

<push_rules>
The remote may enforce push rules on the branch name and on the commit message. A rule reported by the remote wins over every guideline above.
- Rejected because of the branch name: choose a name that satisfies the reported rule, rename with `branch -m <new-name>`, and push again.
- Rejected because of the commit message: rewrite the message so it satisfies the reported rule, amend the commit with `commit --amend -m "<message>"`, and push again.
- Failed for any other reason: stop and report the failure.
- At most 5 push attempts in total.
</push_rules>

Finish with the structured output tool: `status` is `success` only when the remote accepted the push, otherwise `failed` with the remote's rejection message verbatim in `error`. `branch_name` MUST be the exact branch you pushed — the merge request is opened against that name.

